2
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:16,730
ago. Support checks the release
notes: the fix is listed under 1.4.1.

3
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:21,816
Engineering checks the code of
1.5.0: the faulty line is there.

4
00:00:21,856 --> 00:00:25,529
Nobody reverted anything.
Nobody force-pushed.

5
00:00:25,869 --> 00:00:30,363
The answer is precise, and you'll be able
to produce it from the repository alone:

6
00:00:30,403 --> 00:00:34,896
the fix was committed on the release branch
and never reached main, and nobody ran

7
00:00:34,936 --> 00:00:39,264
the one-line check that would have listed
it. Keep that one-line check in mind.

8
00:00:39,304 --> 00:00:41,365
You'll watch it print the missing fix.

9
00:00:43,205 --> 00:00:47,183
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:47,223 --> 00:00:52,585
In video 171 you saw the models as drawings.
Today the same project is run through

11
00:00:52,625 --> 00:00:57,592
one release and one hotfix twice, with
real commits, and you compare the two

12
00:00:57,632 --> 00:01:02,928
histories by asking the tags. A tag is a
name fixed on one commit, and here every

13
00:01:02,968 --> 00:01:07,803
release is a tag. A hotfix is an urgent
fix to something already released.

14
00:01:08,143 --> 00:01:12,108
The scenario is fixed.
promptgate 1.3.0 is released.

15
00:01:12,148 --> 00:01:16,113
Two pull requests are merged,
and 1.4.0 is released.

16
00:01:16,153 --> 00:01:20,811
A third pull request, a batch
endpoint, is merged afterwards.

17
00:01:20,851 --> 00:01:27,049
Then production reports that suspended
tenants, whose limit is 0, aren't blocked,

18
00:01:27,089 --> 00:01:29,282
and a fix must ship as 1.4.1.

19
00:01:29,622 --> 00:01:34,776
The second half is about direction. Once
there are two lines of development, a fix

20
00:01:34,816 --> 00:01:39,716
has to exist on both, and there are two
legitimate conventions for how it gets

21
00:01:39,756 --> 00:01:44,973
there. They're opposites. Each gives you
an invariant, a statement that must always

22
00:01:45,013 --> 00:01:50,229
hold, and one command can test it. From
video 63 you know cherry-pick, -x and patch

23
00:01:50,269 --> 00:01:52,700
IDs, which carry the second convention.

24
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:58,148
A cherry-pick copies one commit's change
onto another branch as a new commit with a

25
00:01:58,188 --> 00:02:02,491
new ID. The -x option records the
original's ID in the copy's message.

26
00:02:02,531 --> 00:02:07,391
And a patch ID is a hash of the change
itself, by which Git recognizes the same

27
00:02:07,431 --> 00:02:09,314
change under another commit ID.

28
00:02:11,154 --> 00:02:12,733
Here's what you'll walk away with.

29
00:02:13,073 --> 00:02:14,485
After this video you can:

30
00:02:14,825 --> 00:02:19,478
Run one release and one hotfix under GitHub
Flow and under a release-branch strategy

31
00:02:19,518 --> 00:02:20,931
and compare the histories.

32
00:02:21,271 --> 00:02:24,265
Say what ships in each case
and prove it from tags.

33
00:02:24,605 --> 00:02:29,217
State the two conventions for the direction
of a fix and the invariant each lets you

34
00:02:29,257 --> 00:02:29,494
test.

35
00:02:29,834 --> 00:02:32,980
Detect a fix that was forgotten
on one line of development.

36
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:37,504
Explain what goes wrong when both
directions are mixed in one repository.

37
00:02:39,344 --> 00:02:43,818
Two strategies, one sentence. The same
three features and the same one-line fix

38
00:02:43,858 --> 00:02:48,332
produce two different answers to "what
does the patch release contain", and the

39
00:02:48,372 --> 00:02:50,732
difference is where the release tag lives.

40
00:02:51,072 --> 00:02:54,624
Under strategy A, GitHub Flow,
releases are tags on main.

41
00:02:54,664 --> 00:02:59,918
A hotfix is the next deployment of main.
Under strategy B there is a release branch.

42
00:02:59,958 --> 00:03:05,149
The fix is made on main first and cherry-picked
down, and the tag is on the release

43
00:03:05,189 --> 00:03:05,590
branch.

44
00:03:05,930 --> 00:03:11,648
Inside .git. Strategy A created two
tag objects and moved refs/heads/main.

45
00:03:11,688 --> 00:03:18,107
Strategy B created the same two tags, one
extra ref refs/heads/release/1.4, and one

46
00:03:18,147 --> 00:03:24,644
extra commit object. A "release" in both is
refs/tags/v1.4.1 naming an annotated tag

47
00:03:24,684 --> 00:03:26,745
object that names a commit.

48
00:03:27,085 --> 00:03:32,727
One sentence from the textbook belongs on
this table: strategy A can become strategy

49
00:03:32,767 --> 00:03:38,408
B on the day it is needed. git switch -c
release/1.4 v1.4.0 creates the missing line

50
00:03:38,448 --> 00:03:43,955
at the tag, after the fact. That's an
argument for starting with the simpler model

51
00:03:43,995 --> 00:03:48,216
and adding release branches when
a second live version appears.

52
00:03:48,556 --> 00:03:52,670
GitHub, not Git. A GitHub release
is an object layered on a Git tag.

53
00:03:52,710 --> 00:03:57,618
Creating one in the web interface or with
gh release create can create the tag on

54
00:03:57,658 --> 00:04:01,528
the server, and your clone doesn't
have it until you fetch tags.

55
00:04:01,568 --> 00:04:06,599
With immutable releases enabled, the tag
and its assets are locked and the tag name

56
00:04:06,639 --> 00:04:07,576
can't be reused.

57
00:04:07,916 --> 00:04:11,989
Which way does a fix travel? There are
two legitimate conventions and they're

58
00:04:12,029 --> 00:04:16,315
opposites: commit the fix on the oldest
branch that needs it and merge upward, or

59
00:04:16,355 --> 00:04:18,878
commit it on main first
and cherry-pick it down.

60
00:04:19,218 --> 00:04:24,112
Merge upward. The Git project's own workflow
document states it: "Always commit your

61
00:04:24,152 --> 00:04:27,401
fixes to the oldest supported
branch that requires them.

62
00:04:27,441 --> 00:04:31,864
Then (periodically) merge the integration
branches upwards into each other."

63
00:04:31,904 --> 00:04:36,798
The fix is one commit with one ID, and the
merge commit is, in that document's word,

64
00:04:36,838 --> 00:04:41,438
a "promise" that everything from the older
branch is included in the newer one.

65
00:04:41,478 --> 00:04:45,373
The invariant: each release branch
is an ancestor of the next line.

66
00:04:45,413 --> 00:04:50,012
Ancestor means that you reach it by
following parents back from the newer line.

67
00:04:50,352 --> 00:04:55,068
Fix on main first, cherry-pick down. The
trunk-based development site, Google's

68
00:04:55,108 --> 00:05:00,065
published practice, Microsoft's Release
Flow and GitLab's rules all prescribe this.

69
00:05:00,105 --> 00:05:05,001
The stated reason is the failure it prevents:
a fix made on the release branch can

70
00:05:05,041 --> 00:05:08,794
be forgotten on the trunk, and
the next release then regresses.

71
00:05:08,834 --> 00:05:13,670
The invariant can't be ancestry, because
the release branch is never merged back.

72
00:05:13,710 --> 00:05:18,607
It's a comparison of patches: every change
on the release branch has an equivalent

73
00:05:18,647 --> 00:05:19,088
on main.

74
00:05:19,428 --> 00:05:23,747
Read the table row by row: the identity
of the fix, the question you can ask, a

75
00:05:23,787 --> 00:05:26,451
forgotten step, the baggage,
and where each fits.

76
00:05:26,791 --> 00:05:30,545
Quick quiz. Under pick down,
somebody forgets the pick.

77
00:05:30,585 --> 00:05:34,269
What happens? A, the next
release ships the bug again.

78
00:05:34,309 --> 00:05:36,615
B, nothing dangerous. Your answer?

79
00:05:41,455 --> 00:05:46,030
B. That was the third row of the table:
the old release lacks a fix it never had.

80
00:05:46,070 --> 00:05:50,588
Under merge upward, the forgotten step is
the merge, and the fix is then missing

81
00:05:50,628 --> 00:05:52,754
from main until the next upward merge.

82
00:05:53,094 --> 00:05:57,937
The limits of the checks. Two, and you
need both for the interview question.

83
00:05:57,977 --> 00:06:02,820
A cherry-pick that needed a conflict
resolution has a different patch ID, so

84
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:06,867
--cherry-pick reports it as missing
although a human ported it.

85
00:06:06,907 --> 00:06:12,007
The -x line is then the evidence. And an
upward merge resolved by discarding the

86
00:06:12,047 --> 00:06:16,762
release branch's side satisfies the
ancestry test while the fix is absent.

87
00:06:16,802 --> 00:06:20,296
git log --remerge-diff shows
what the resolver changed.

88
00:06:22,136 --> 00:06:26,571
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
a textbook in print in two editions.

89
00:06:26,611 --> 00:06:31,279
You can correct the older edition and let
the correction flow into the newer one.

90
00:06:31,319 --> 00:06:36,045
Or you can correct the current manuscript
and copy the correction by hand into the

91
00:06:36,085 --> 00:06:36,858
older edition.

92
00:06:37,198 --> 00:06:42,249
The first is merge upward: one correction,
and the newer edition includes the older

93
00:06:42,289 --> 00:06:47,278
one whole. The second is pick down: two
corrections that say the same thing, and a

94
00:06:47,318 --> 00:06:50,958
note in the margin of the copy
that says where it came from.

95
00:06:50,998 --> 00:06:52,491
That note is the -x line.

96
00:06:52,831 --> 00:06:57,435
Where the analogy breaks: Git can do the
first mechanically, with a merge, only

97
00:06:57,475 --> 00:07:01,255
while the newer line still contains
the older one as an ancestor.

98
00:07:01,295 --> 00:07:05,958
Once a line has stopped being an ancestor,
or once the two conventions have been

99
00:07:05,998 --> 00:07:08,779
mixed, the merge is no
longer a clean inclusion.

100
00:07:09,119 --> 00:07:14,171
And one sentence to keep under all of it,
from the root-cause box: nothing in Git

101
00:07:14,211 --> 00:07:18,258
propagates a fix between branches.
Branches are independent refs.

102
00:07:18,298 --> 00:07:23,476
"This fix belongs everywhere" is knowledge
held by the team, not by the repository.

103
00:07:25,316 --> 00:07:28,626
Two release lines and main,
first with upward merges.

104
00:07:28,666 --> 00:07:33,873
The fix F is committed once, on the oldest
line. Each merge carries it one line up.

105
00:07:34,213 --> 00:07:39,222
Then with downward picks. F is committed
on main. The two copies, F prime and F

106
00:07:39,262 --> 00:07:42,674
double prime, are separate
commits with their own IDs.

107
00:07:42,714 --> 00:07:48,042
The marks aren't in the repository. They
stand for "same change", which Git can only

108
00:07:48,082 --> 00:07:51,174
infer from the patch ID
or read from the -x line.

109
00:07:53,014 --> 00:07:56,709
Into the lab, for four replays.
Strategy A comes first.

110
00:07:57,049 --> 00:08:01,393
Step 1: strategy A, the release.
Two pull requests are merged.

111
00:08:01,433 --> 00:08:05,070
main is deployable, so the
release is a tag on main.

112
00:08:05,110 --> 00:08:08,818
🟢 SAFE: an annotated tag
adds a tag object and a ref.

113
00:08:09,158 --> 00:08:14,115
Step 2: main moves on. Pull request
43 lands after the release.

114
00:08:14,455 --> 00:08:17,841
main is ahead of what customers
run, by the batch endpoint.

115
00:08:18,181 --> 00:08:22,101
Step 3: the hotfix. 🟡
CAUTION: a merge moves main.

116
00:08:25,441 --> 00:08:30,895
Look at the diff first. The bug is the or:
a limit of 0 is falsy in Python, so the

117
00:08:30,935 --> 00:08:35,517
suspended tenant silently got the
default limit. The fix is one line.

118
00:08:35,857 --> 00:08:42,891
Step 4: what ships. Predict before the
output: what does a customer get when moving

119
00:08:42,931 --> 00:08:46,215
from v1.4.0 to v1.4.1? Say it out loud.

120
00:08:51,055 --> 00:08:56,975
Two commits and two files. Version 1.4.1
contains the fix and the batch endpoint.

121
00:08:57,315 --> 00:09:02,275
Under GitHub Flow that is by design: main
is what you ship, and everything merged

122
00:09:02,315 --> 00:09:07,090
since the last tag ships with the next
one. For a hosted service with one live

123
00:09:07,130 --> 00:09:11,906
version this is fine, and usually desirable.
For a customer who pinned 1.4 and

124
00:09:11,946 --> 00:09:16,844
expects a patch release to contain only
fixes, it's a breach of what the version

125
00:09:16,884 --> 00:09:20,918
number promised: Semantic Versioning
reserves the third number for

126
00:09:20,958 --> 00:09:22,770
backward-compatible bug fixes.

127
00:09:23,110 --> 00:09:27,471
Step 5: strategy B. Replay
labs/run ch27/release-branch.

128
00:09:27,511 --> 00:09:33,443
The same two pull requests are merged.
The release gets a branch of its own.

129
00:09:33,783 --> 00:09:38,516
main moves on with the batch endpoint, as
before. The fix is made and reviewed on

130
00:09:38,556 --> 00:09:39,164
main first.

131
00:09:39,504 --> 00:09:44,637
The fix on main is bc59804. Now it is
copied down. 🟡 CAUTION: git cherry-pick -x

132
00:09:44,677 --> 00:09:49,940
<commit> creates a new commit on the current
branch with the same change and moves

133
00:09:49,980 --> 00:09:51,039
the branch to it.

134
00:09:51,379 --> 00:09:55,514
The last lines are the message
of the new commit, 996796d.

135
00:09:55,554 --> 00:10:01,559
The -x added "(cherry picked from commit
...)". That line is the only durable record

136
00:10:01,599 --> 00:10:06,742
that 996796d on the release branch
and bc59804 on main are the same fix.

137
00:10:07,082 --> 00:10:09,785
Step 6: what ships, and where the fix is.

138
00:10:10,125 --> 00:10:16,237
One commit, one file. Version 1.4.1
contains the fix and nothing else.

139
00:10:16,277 --> 00:10:19,577
The batch endpoint will ship in 1.5.0.

140
00:10:19,917 --> 00:10:26,376
git branch --contains gives a different
answer for each ID: one says main, the other

141
00:10:26,416 --> 00:10:27,691
says release/1.4.

142
00:10:28,031 --> 00:10:33,158
The fix exists as two commits. Git can
still pair them, because they introduce the

143
00:10:33,198 --> 00:10:36,561
same change: the two lines
marked with an equals sign.

144
00:10:36,901 --> 00:10:43,085
Try it now, for thirty seconds. In any
repository you have, type git log --oneline,

145
00:10:43,125 --> 00:10:47,359
copy one ID, and type git
branch --contains with that ID.

146
00:10:47,399 --> 00:10:49,983
Both commands only read. I'll wait.

147
00:10:54,823 --> 00:10:58,587
The answer lists the branches from
which that commit can be reached.

148
00:10:58,627 --> 00:11:02,055
It's the question "is the fix in
this line?", asked of one ID.

149
00:11:02,395 --> 00:11:09,058
Step 7: the two conventions side by side.
Replay labs/run ch27/fix-direction.

150
00:11:09,098 --> 00:11:16,369
The starting state is the same in all three
parts: release/1.4 at the 1.4.0 tag, and

151
00:11:16,409 --> 00:11:20,808
main one feature ahead.
Convention A, merge upward.

152
00:11:29,148 --> 00:11:32,915
One commit, 64610e3,
contained in both branches.

153
00:11:33,254 --> 00:11:39,388
git merge-base --is-ancestor release/1.4
main exits with status 0, and an exit

154
00:11:39,428 --> 00:11:44,057
status is something a CI job,
an automated check, can test.

155
00:11:44,397 --> 00:11:49,746
Convention B, pick down. Predict: what
will the same ancestry test return here?

156
00:11:49,786 --> 00:11:50,838
Say it out loud.

157
00:12:03,678 --> 00:12:09,312
Two commits, d9474f7 and a2d7a91.
The ancestry test fails by design.

158
00:12:09,652 --> 00:12:14,851
What replaces it is git log --cherry-pick
--right-only main...release/1.4, which

159
00:12:14,891 --> 00:12:20,024
lists the commits on the release branch
that have no equivalent change on main.

160
00:12:20,064 --> 00:12:25,460
Empty means nothing is missing. The manual
describes --cherry-pick as omitting "any

161
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:30,830
commit that introduces the same change as
another commit on the 'other side'" of a

162
00:12:30,870 --> 00:12:35,283
symmetric difference, and Git decides
"the same change" by patch ID.

163
00:12:35,323 --> 00:12:40,522
If the two checks blur together at first,
that's normal: one tests ancestry, the

164
00:12:40,562 --> 00:12:42,028
other compares patches.

165
00:12:42,368 --> 00:12:46,131
Step 8: the failure both
conventions exist to prevent.

166
00:12:46,171 --> 00:12:51,766
A fix made only on the release branch.
Weeks later release 1.5 is cut from main.

167
00:12:52,106 --> 00:12:57,271
The two git show lines are the hook of
this video: v1.5.0 has the or again.

168
00:12:57,311 --> 00:13:00,463
Predict: what will each
of the two checks say?

169
00:13:05,303 --> 00:13:11,225
The ancestry test exits with 1. The patch
comparison prints one line, and that line

170
00:13:11,265 --> 00:13:13,596
names the forgotten fix: 70188ed.

171
00:13:13,936 --> 00:13:18,261
There's the one-line check from the opening.
Either check finds it before the tag.

172
00:13:18,601 --> 00:13:24,984
The root-cause box has the "Correct fix"
line: port the commit, release 1.5.1, and

173
00:13:25,024 --> 00:13:29,528
don't move the v1.5.0 tag,
because people already have it.

174
00:13:29,868 --> 00:13:34,882
Step 9: mixing the directions. Replay
labs/run ch27/mixed-directions.

175
00:13:34,922 --> 00:13:39,204
The first fix went from main
to release/1.4 by cherry-pick.

176
00:13:39,244 --> 00:13:45,284
A second fix, on the neighbouring line of
the same function, is made on the release

177
00:13:45,324 --> 00:13:45,797
branch.

178
00:13:46,137 --> 00:13:50,334
Then somebody carries it to main by
merging the release branch upward.

179
00:13:50,374 --> 00:13:54,510
Predict: main never touched the
second line. Will the merge be clean?

180
00:13:59,350 --> 00:14:03,871
A conflict. The upward merge brought the
picked copy of the first fix with it.

181
00:14:03,911 --> 00:14:08,549
Relative to the merge base, main changed
one line and the release branch changed

182
00:14:08,589 --> 00:14:12,584
that line identically plus its
neighbour, so the two changes overlap.

183
00:14:12,624 --> 00:14:17,438
Git dropped the identical line from the
conflict and left the neighbouring line for

184
00:14:17,478 --> 00:14:19,777
a human, although main never touched it.

185
00:14:20,117 --> 00:14:23,663
git merge --abort returns to
the state before the merge.

186
00:14:23,703 --> 00:14:28,786
A repository that mixes the directions
satisfies neither check of this video and

187
00:14:28,826 --> 00:14:31,284
pays for it in conflicts like this one.

188
00:14:33,124 --> 00:14:34,772
Five mistakes to watch for.

189
00:14:35,112 --> 00:14:38,305
Promising a fix-only patch
release under GitHub Flow.

190
00:14:38,345 --> 00:14:43,248
Root cause: the release tag lives on main,
so a patch release contains everything

191
00:14:43,288 --> 00:14:45,078
merged since the previous tag.

192
00:14:45,418 --> 00:14:48,781
Committing a fix on the release
branch and stopping there.

193
00:14:48,821 --> 00:14:53,709
Root cause: nothing in Git propagates a fix
between branches; carrying it to main is

194
00:14:53,749 --> 00:14:54,472
a human step.

195
00:14:54,812 --> 00:14:59,477
Cherry-picking without -x. Root cause:
the copy has a new ID, and without the

196
00:14:59,517 --> 00:15:04,489
recorded line the link between the two
commits exists only as a matching patch ID,

197
00:15:04,529 --> 00:15:06,750
which a conflict resolution destroys.

198
00:15:07,090 --> 00:15:11,459
Trusting the patch-ID check after a
port that needed conflict resolution.

199
00:15:11,499 --> 00:15:16,231
Root cause: the resolved diff differs
from the original, so the check reports a

200
00:15:16,271 --> 00:15:17,559
ported fix as missing.

201
00:15:17,899 --> 00:15:20,822
Mixing merge-upward and
pick-down in one repository.

202
00:15:20,862 --> 00:15:25,267
Root cause: an upward merge brings the
picked copies with it, so identical and

203
00:15:25,307 --> 00:15:28,515
neighbouring changes overlap
and neither invariant holds.

204
00:15:30,355 --> 00:15:36,423
Now, out of the lab. A team ships an
inference SDK with two supported versions and

205
00:15:36,463 --> 00:15:42,456
uses the pick-down convention: fix on main,
cherry-pick with -x to each supported

206
00:15:42,496 --> 00:15:48,564
release branch. During an urgent customer
escalation an engineer fixes a tokenizer

207
00:15:48,604 --> 00:15:52,587
bug directly on release/2.3,
tags 2.3.4 and goes home.

208
00:15:52,627 --> 00:15:55,492
Six weeks later 2.4.0 is cut from main.

209
00:15:55,832 --> 00:16:01,806
This time nothing regresses, because the
team had made the check a release gate

