234
00:18:03,012 --> 00:18:05,062
Let's land this, in your own words.

235
00:18:05,402 --> 00:18:10,082
Under GitHub Flow a patch release is whatever
main contains at the next tag; with a

236
00:18:10,122 --> 00:18:13,438
release branch it is only what
was picked onto that branch.

237
00:18:13,778 --> 00:18:24,969
git log --no-merges v1.4.0..v1.4.1 and
git diff --stat v1.4.0 v1.4.1 prove what

238
00:18:25,009 --> 00:18:26,107
shipped.

239
00:18:26,447 --> 00:18:30,858
Merge upward gives one commit and the
invariant "the release branch is an ancestor

240
00:18:30,898 --> 00:18:35,255
of main"; pick down gives one commit per
line and the invariant "no change on the

241
00:18:35,295 --> 00:18:37,644
release branch lacks an
equivalent on main".

242
00:18:37,984 --> 00:18:42,916
A forgotten fix is found before the tag by
either check; afterwards you port it and

243
00:18:42,956 --> 00:18:45,132
release again without moving the tag.

244
00:18:45,472 --> 00:18:50,288
Mixing the two directions breaks both
invariants and produces conflicts on lines

245
00:18:50,328 --> 00:18:51,563
nobody changed twice.

